// A NEW DEBATE MAP. Everything below is a placeholder — replace it with the debate you are
// surveying. Lines beginning with // are comments and never reach the map.
//
// A debate map surveys a pattern of public argument — the case for and against a claim —
// rather than reading one text. So no claim cites a source, and none carries a fidelity
// border: there is no text to stand at a distance from. What the map owes instead is
// fairness: each side's best case, laid out step by step where anyone can examine it. Put
// your name in the front matter, because a debate map stands on who drew it.
//
// Tags — #pro and #con below, but any names, and any number of sides — mark which standpoint
// a claim or argument belongs to.
//
// Reading one text instead? File ▸ New Reconstruction starts from the other skeleton, where
// every claim can cite its source.
//
// Press ? or "How to use" for the rest.

===
title: A new debate map
author: Your Name
date: 2026
===

[main-claim]: The claim the debate turns on. #pro
    + <the-case-for>
    - [the-objection]: The strongest consideration on the other side, as its proponents actually press it. #con
        + <why-it-presses>

<the-case-for>: How the best case for the claim runs, spelled out step by step. #pro

    (1) [first-reason]: The first premise, in the strongest form it circulates in.
    (2) [second-reason]: The premise it needs alongside the first.
    ----
    (3) [main-claim]

<why-it-presses>: Why the objection is not a quibble — the reasoning behind it. #con

    (1) [rival-ground]: What the other side starts from.
    ----
    (2) [the-objection]
